WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.340 --> 00:00:04.300
Throughout the years you have seen me
chop with many weird items

00:00:09.800 --> 00:00:12.920
Some of you may know that in table tennis there are long pimple rubbers

00:00:12.920 --> 00:00:15.480
that are usually used with a defensive playing style

00:00:15.720 --> 00:00:19.820
When chopping, the pimples generate a great amount of underspin on topspin shots

00:00:20.640 --> 00:00:23.500
But how difficult would it be to learn to play with a long pimple rubber

00:00:23.500 --> 00:00:26.780
and switch your playing style from offensive to defensive?

00:00:28.720 --> 00:00:30.580
Would it turn out to be a success

00:00:31.300 --> 00:00:33.100
or a complete fail?

00:00:37.040 --> 00:00:39.400
Okay so now I have a brand new table tennis racket

00:00:39.400 --> 00:00:41.860
I have the long pimple on the red side

00:00:41.860 --> 00:00:45.500
and this is gonna be extremely interesting to try this out

00:00:45.500 --> 00:00:47.920
and see if I can do any chops with this

00:00:48.260 --> 00:00:50.940
So here begins the one-year chopping challenge

00:00:51.920 --> 00:00:56.640
In this video I'll share my thoughts on how changing my playing style has felt so far

00:00:57.900 --> 00:01:01.560
One of the most difficult things in the beginning was the basic push

00:01:03.320 --> 00:01:06.020
There you see the difficult part

00:01:06.020 --> 00:01:08.160
trying to push with the long pimple

00:01:08.160 --> 00:01:11.680
If it's a backspin it doesn't react the same way as a normal rubber

00:01:11.680 --> 00:01:14.000
so you have to hit higher to make it on table

00:01:14.020 --> 00:01:18.540
Pushing with the long pimple requires a different technique than pushing with an inverted rubber

00:01:18.540 --> 00:01:22.760
so in the beginning pretty much all my long pimple pushes went in to the net

00:01:23.120 --> 00:01:27.140
That just proves how important it is to get the basic push first

00:01:27.140 --> 00:01:29.420
because if I can't get that on table

00:01:30.000 --> 00:01:31.860
Then the points are very short

00:01:32.400 --> 00:01:33.920
Try pushing a few

00:01:35.080 --> 00:01:37.500
As you can see in this clip pushing with the long pimple

00:01:37.500 --> 00:01:40.420
feels very different to pushing with the normal rubber

00:01:43.220 --> 00:01:44.060
Yeah it's different

00:01:44.540 --> 00:01:47.240
This is the real shot you need to practise

00:01:47.240 --> 00:01:51.520
The most underrated and the most boring but probably the most important

00:01:51.520 --> 00:01:52.540
Yeah exactly!

00:01:52.540 --> 00:01:56.540
I also realized that pushing with the long pimple requires a longer stroke

00:01:56.540 --> 00:01:59.960
because the ball doesn't grip to the pimples as it does to normal rubber

00:02:02.760 --> 00:02:06.280
During the first weeks everything felt
really difficult

00:02:09.860 --> 00:02:13.180
It was just very difficult to find any consistency with the chopping

00:02:17.660 --> 00:02:21.160
And sometimes having the pimple rubber completely messed me up

00:02:21.580 --> 00:02:22.640
What am I doing?

00:02:22.860 --> 00:02:23.460
That was a block!

00:02:23.480 --> 00:02:25.080
So used to blocking it

00:02:25.400 --> 00:02:27.100
And as you can see here

00:02:31.820 --> 00:02:35.440
having to twiddle the racket was another thing that I wasn’t used to

00:02:38.480 --> 00:02:42.120
But at the same time, I had never before even tried playing with long pips

00:02:42.120 --> 00:02:45.300
so it was also very interesting and a lot of fun to try it out!

00:02:55.400 --> 00:02:58.200
When I eventually started to get a bit of feeling for the chopping

00:02:58.200 --> 00:03:01.220
the next problem was that the chops were often too high

00:03:01.760 --> 00:03:02.820
So high!

00:03:03.280 --> 00:03:06.480
So it became very easy for Otto to just hit hard

00:03:06.480 --> 00:03:07.720
and win the point.

00:03:09.140 --> 00:03:12.680
So the thing I have to learn is to try to keep the chops lower above the net

00:03:12.680 --> 00:03:16.280
and also place them better to make
it more difficult for the opponent

00:03:17.520 --> 00:03:20.920
Another thing that is important to
remember is that being a chopper

00:03:20.920 --> 00:03:22.920
means that you have to be patient

00:03:23.700 --> 00:03:26.480
This is something that is really difficult to keep in mind

00:03:26.480 --> 00:03:29.780
since I've been playing with an offensive playing style basically my whole life

00:03:29.780 --> 00:03:31.160
before these two months

00:03:32.020 --> 00:03:34.680
As an offensive player you want to be in control of the game

00:03:34.680 --> 00:03:36.040
and make the shots yourself

00:03:36.640 --> 00:03:39.420
But as a chopper you are doing pretty much the opposite

00:03:39.420 --> 00:03:43.220
just trying to return the ball on table and waiting for the opponent to make errors

00:03:47.180 --> 00:03:51.700
I also feel like I don't yet have the consistency to get so many chops on table in a row

00:03:52.520 --> 00:03:55.460
so I often think that it's more likely for me to get a point

00:03:55.460 --> 00:03:58.540
by trying to counter attack the topspin rather than chop it

00:03:59.160 --> 00:04:00.840
But if I wan't to improve this style

00:04:00.840 --> 00:04:03.540
I have to start finding the confidence in my chops

00:04:18.920 --> 00:04:21.980
Being a chopper, the movement is also very important

00:04:23.020 --> 00:04:25.260
Here is maybe the most challenging part

00:04:25.260 --> 00:04:29.040
that being able to move fast from here to here

00:04:29.060 --> 00:04:32.360
to be able to get the long and the fast shot

00:04:32.360 --> 00:04:35.380
So a lot of good footwork is required

00:04:35.380 --> 00:04:36.640
which I don't have yet

00:04:37.380 --> 00:04:40.880
So I've been practicing my chops with regular exercises for a while now

00:04:40.880 --> 00:04:43.740
and tried to focus on the technique and
the basics

00:04:43.740 --> 00:04:46.460
Let's see if I can apply anything of it in a real match

00:04:46.880 --> 00:04:49.880
It's going to be interesting to see the 
difference with this set

00:04:49.880 --> 00:04:51.480
compared to like after one year

00:04:52.100 --> 00:04:53.740
This is probably gonna be horrible

00:05:06.580 --> 00:05:09.120
Here you can clearly see the problem
with the pushing

00:05:18.300 --> 00:05:20.000
As you can see in this one game match,

00:05:20.000 --> 00:05:23.720
everything becomes much more difficult when you don’t know where the ball is coming

00:05:24.040 --> 00:05:28.520
You don’t really know what to do with the pimple, where to move and when to go for an attack

00:05:30.460 --> 00:05:33.300
It’s just more difficult than in regular exercises

00:05:33.300 --> 00:05:36.500
and also shows why you should do irregular exercises in training.

00:06:21.720 --> 00:06:25.340
So in short, you could say that it’s been a quite challenging journey this far

00:06:25.340 --> 00:06:28.900
and I’ve already several times, especially after playing some matches,

00:06:28.900 --> 00:06:31.360
wanted to change back to an inverted rubber.

00:06:33.080 --> 00:06:38.020
It’s sometimes hard to remember that it takes a lot of time and practice to learn a new playing style

00:06:38.020 --> 00:06:41.780
and reach even the same level that you had with the previous playing style

00:06:42.900 --> 00:06:45.660
It’s a long process and requires much more training

00:06:47.860 --> 00:06:49.000
Don't return it!

00:06:49.760 --> 00:06:51.760
Why do you hit it straight to my racket

00:06:52.340 --> 00:06:53.580
My fault yeah sorry

00:06:55.460 --> 00:06:59.320
But I also have to say that its been really exciting to try a new aspect of the sport

00:06:59.320 --> 00:07:01.460
and get to understand choppers better

00:07:01.660 --> 00:07:05.000
I think it’s a very cool and fun playing style when you get better at it

00:07:05.000 --> 00:07:07.140
so I’ll keep on fighting and try to improve more.

00:07:12.100 --> 00:07:13.860
I call it the dynamite!

00:07:13.860 --> 00:07:14.820
The dynamite shot!

00:07:18.860 --> 00:07:19.800
Dynamite!

